{"review_id":"00001788454764715784-0001485283-000001-cycle-review-1","reviewed_manifest_hash":"sha256:49868bfa1fe7733cc0afe6f5b930811244b934478f8407c633d38e712826407b","findings":[{"finding_id":"f1-daemon-tests-unverified","category":"test_gap","severity":"high","blocking":false,"title":"The new batch-review tests are exercised only by the failing, non-required workspace check","claim":"The 941-line integration suite added at crates/familiar-ai-daemon/tests/batch_review.rs is the primary evidence for acceptance criteria 1, 2, 3, and 5, but no required verification check compiles or runs the familiar-ai-daemon crate's tests: 'tests-green-crates' is scoped to -p familiar-ai-core -p familiar-ai-review -p familiar-ai-agent -p familiar-ai-storage, and the only check that would run these tests, 'tests-workspace-advisory', is marked required=false and failed with exit code 101. The package therefore contains no passing evidence that the batch submission/park, crash-resume, expiry-fallback, exactly-once, or accounting tests actually pass at this revision.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/tests/batch_review.rs","range":{"start":1,"end":40}},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-workspace-advisory","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:8668e687ab577bc98e55a37cd66a05e44d5d4ab096aa8298727211348d91f0a8","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":100721,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788454454062745-0001485283-000000/PRD-71","revision":"sha256:1a303eb9f2ed7d11d6fad3b5070b5385ad53b52c4be8690e4418a2fc106acf94","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-8668e687ab577bc98e55a37cd66a05e44d5d4ab096aa8298727211348d91f0a8-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Either add -p familiar-ai-daemon to the required 'tests-green-crates' check so the new suite gates the change, or resolve the workspace-advisory failure and promote that check to required, so the acceptance-criteria evidence rests on a passing gate rather than a failing advisory one.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"f2-scoped-policy-replaces-global","category":"correctness_defect","severity":"high","blocking":false,"title":"`batch-review enable` writes a partial repository-scoped tier_policy that replaces, not merges, the global policy","claim":"`enable` creates `repositories.<key>.review.tier_policy` containing only `batch_risk_classes`, `max_batch_wait_ms`, and `batch_worker` (tier_policy_table + the three inserts). `resolved_tier_policy` then prefers that repository entry wholesale — `config.repositories.get(key)...tier_policy.as_ref().or(config.review.tier_policy.as_ref())` — with no merge of the global policy's fields. Because `ReviewTierPolicyConfig` derives every remaining field from `#[serde(default)]`, a repository that previously inherited a global policy with `independent_review_required = true`, populated `full_review_risk_classes`, and footprint `rules` silently reverts all three to their defaults for that repository the moment an operator enables batch for one low-risk class. That is exactly the silent downgrade of independent-review and latency guarantees acceptance criterion 6 forbids, and the audit record only shows that a batch class was added.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/cli/batch_review.rs","range":{"start":150,"end":188}},{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/batch_review.rs","range":{"start":380,"end":420}},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:c03c30989c591781013063e4b9e1d742d75942dc9159837a0213a22089908b08","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":38454,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788454454062745-0001485283-000000/PRD-71","revision":"sha256:1a303eb9f2ed7d11d6fad3b5070b5385ad53b52c4be8690e4418a2fc106acf94","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-c03c30989c591781013063e4b9e1d742d75942dc9159837a0213a22089908b08-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Have `enable` copy the effective (global) tier_policy fields into the repository-scoped table before adding the batch keys, or make policy resolution merge repository overrides onto the global policy field-by-field instead of substituting the whole table; add a test asserting that enabling batch for one class preserves the repository's `independent_review_required`, `full_review_risk_classes`, and `rules`.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"f3-submission-not-durable-before-provider-call","category":"correctness_defect","severity":"medium","blocking":false,"title":"A batch is submitted to the provider before any durable row exists, so a crash in that window loses and re-bills it","claim":"`BatchReviewAgent::submit` calls `client.submit_message_batch(...)` and only afterwards writes the `batch_reviews` row via `repository.submit(&NewBatchReview{ ... provider_batch_id: &batch.id ... })`. A daemon crash (or a database error on the insert, which is mapped to `ReviewExecutionError::Agent`) between those two statements leaves a live provider batch with no durable record. The next review cycle finds no row via `find_by_review_id`, takes the `else { return self.submit(...) }` path, and submits a second batch for the same review_id; the first batch is never polled, never expires through `mark_expired_fallback`, and never produces an accounting observation. This contradicts acceptance criterion 3's requirement that the daemon 'never loses a submitted batch', and the crash-resume test only covers the window after the row exists.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/batch_review.rs","range":{"start":180,"end":230}},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:c03c30989c591781013063e4b9e1d742d75942dc9159837a0213a22089908b08","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":38454,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788454454062745-0001485283-000000/PRD-71","revision":"sha256:1a303eb9f2ed7d11d6fad3b5070b5385ad53b52c4be8690e4418a2fc106acf94","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-c03c30989c591781013063e4b9e1d742d75942dc9159837a0213a22089908b08-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Write an intent row (state 'submitting', with the deterministic custom_id) before calling the provider and update it with the returned provider_batch_id afterwards; on resume, reconcile a 'submitting' row against the provider's batch list by custom_id rather than issuing a fresh submission.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"f4-poller-startup-gate-and-early-return","category":"correctness_defect","severity":"medium","blocking":false,"title":"The poller decides once at startup whether to run, and aborts the whole sweep on a single row's transport failure","claim":"`run` evaluates `any_batch_configured(&config)` against the config snapshot captured when the daemon started and returns permanently when it is false. Because `familiar-ai batch-review enable` is an on-disk config mutation performed while the daemon is running, a repository enabled after daemon start has a poller that has already exited: submitted batches are never polled, never marked `expired_fallback`, and the bounded wait of acceptance criterion 5 is not enforced until the daemon is restarted. Separately, inside `poll_once` the per-row failures for `AnthropicHttpClient::new` and `EnvCredentialResolver.resolve` use `return` rather than `continue`, so one repository's missing credential silently abandons every remaining pending row in that tick, including rows already past their deadline.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/batch_review.rs","range":{"start":600,"end":700}},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:c03c30989c591781013063e4b9e1d742d75942dc9159837a0213a22089908b08","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":38454,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788454454062745-0001485283-000000/PRD-71","revision":"sha256:1a303eb9f2ed7d11d6fad3b5070b5385ad53b52c4be8690e4418a2fc106acf94","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-c03c30989c591781013063e4b9e1d742d75942dc9159837a0213a22089908b08-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Keep the poller alive unconditionally and re-read the effective config (or subscribe to config reloads) on each tick, treating `any_batch_configured` as a per-tick skip rather than a permanent exit; change the two per-row `return`s in `poll_once` to `continue` so one repository's failure cannot suppress deadline enforcement for the others. Add a test that a row past its deadline is marked `expired_fallback` even when an earlier row's credential resolution fails.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"f5-assumed-discount-not-provider-reported","category":"correctness_defect","severity":"medium","blocking":false,"title":"Batch cost is an assumed 50% discount on configured interactive rates, not the provider-reported cost the criterion requires","claim":"Acceptance criterion 4 requires each batch execution's accounting observation to record 'the provider-reported cost with provider provenance, so PRD-051 comparisons measure the actual discount ... rather than assuming a rate'. `batch_cost_estimate` does exactly the opposite: it calls `crate::run::calculate_cost` with the configured interactive rates and divides by the hard-coded `BATCH_PRICE_DISCOUNT_DENOMINATOR = 2`. `record_accounting` then sets `provider_cost_lexical: None` on the ledger observation and routes the derived number through `append_legacy_configured_estimate`. The same assumed figure is written into `batch_reviews.provider_cost_lexical`, a column whose name asserts provider provenance for a value the provider never reported. A PRD-051 comparison built on these rows measures the constant 2, not the actual discount; if Anthropic's batch pricing changes, or the batch member consumes cache reads priced differently, the ledger silently reports the wrong cost.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/batch_review.rs","range":{"start":40,"end":100}},{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-storage/migrations/058_batch_reviews.sql","range":{"start":1,"end":31}},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:c03c30989c591781013063e4b9e1d742d75942dc9159837a0213a22089908b08","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":38454,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788454454062745-0001485283-000000/PRD-71","revision":"sha256:1a303eb9f2ed7d11d6fad3b5070b5385ad53b52c4be8690e4418a2fc106acf94","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-c03c30989c591781013063e4b9e1d742d75942dc9159837a0213a22089908b08-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Record the provider-reported figure when one is available (batch usage/cost fields, or reconciliation against the provider's billing surface as PRD-051's provider-cost path already does) and keep the derived number strictly as an estimate: rename the durable column away from `provider_cost_lexical` (or add an explicit provenance column distinguishing 'provider-reported' from 'batch-rate-estimate'), and make the discount factor configuration rather than a compile-time constant.","status":"open","supersedes":null}]}
