Everything is green. Here's a summary of the remediation:

**Finding f2 (tier_policy replaces, not merges, on `batch-review enable`)** — already fully fixed in the working tree (`crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/cli/batch_review.rs`, `crates/familiar-ai-core/src/config/review.rs`): `enable` now resolves the *effective* policy (repo override, falling back to global) via `effective_tier_policy`/`resolved_tier_policy`, seeds a freshly created repo-scoped `tier_policy` table from it via `seed_tier_policy_item`, and only then adds the batch-specific keys — so `independent_review_required`, `full_review_risk_classes`, and `rules` survive. Two regression tests (`effective_tier_policy_falls_back_to_the_global_policy`, `enabling_batch_for_one_class_preserves_inherited_tier_policy_fields`) cover it, and both pass. I found and fixed one real defect while verifying this: the file was **not `cargo fmt`-clean**, which would have failed the `format` check — fixed with `cargo fmt --all`.

**Finding f1 (no required check exercises the daemon's new tests)** — I could not remediate this within scope. Both suggested fixes (add `-p familiar-ai-daemon` to `tests-green-crates`, or promote `tests-workspace-advisory` to required) require editing the verification-plan/check configuration, and I confirmed by grepping the entire repo that no such config exists anywhere in this repository — it's owned entirely by the external review orchestrator, outside `allowed_paths: ["crates/"]`. I investigated the third option (resolve the advisory check's actual failure) and traced it to `crates/familiar-ai-daemon/tests/integration.rs::daemon_starts_and_stops_on_sigterm`, which fails deterministically in the Docker test environment — but I verified this **also fails identically at the unmodified base revision `e82f885`**, before any PRD-71 code exists. It's a pre-existing, unrelated daemon SIGTERM-handling flake, not something these two findings ask me to fix, and "fixing" it would be scope creep beyond the two blocking findings. All of the actual batch-review evidence is solid on its own: `crates/familiar-ai-daemon/tests/batch_review.rs` (9 tests), the daemon crate's full suite (132+ tests), and the entire non-Docker `cargo test --workspace` all pass cleanly.
